1. `rand() % n + 1` : use this to generate random whole numbers and this n will 
    decide upper limit and here upper limit is (n-1) and then this + 1 will decide 
    lower limit if n is 5 then lower limit is (0+1) and upper limit is (5-1+1) i.e. 5.
    
    
2. `Now how we can use this to find the test case where our code is stucking ` : first 
    write a brute force for that solution or pickup someone's accepted solution and then 
    for both make a function like correctanswer for that brute force(assuming your brute 
    force approach is correct) or someone accepted solution then general format :
		 1. `Ex` : constraints : 1 <= T <= 10; 1 <= N <= 100000; 1 <= a[i] <= 1000000; 
			srand(time(NULL));
			int cnt = 1;
			while(cnt <= 1000){
			int n = rand() % 5 +1;
			for(int i = 0 ; i < n; i++){
			a[i] = rand() % 7 + 1;
			int correct_ans = function_of_correct_ans(a,n);
			int my_solution= my_solution_function(a,n);
			}
			if(correct_ans != my_solution){
			for(int i =0 ; i < n; i++){
			cout << a[i] << ' ' ;}
			cout << '\n';
			break;
			}
			cnt++;
			}
			we take small sample of 1000 test cases and all sample of small array if you
			want big size of array you can do % 10 or any number you want to check our answe
			r and at whatever my answer with differ with correct answer i will just output that
			test case and then i can debug my solution accordingly . Smaller test case is better 
			to debug our code easier and in a faster way.This big cp players do because writing 
			brute force solution is easier than writing optimised solution. And by this they find 
			the edge case .
			
			
